Regarding the idea to have some macros that expand to null when unwanted -- I have some &quot;developer&quot; macros that, in day-to-day use, do things like showing TODO items.<p>The most useful one typesets the labels of equations, sections, figures, and so forth, so I can copy-paste right from the document as I&#x27;m editing.  That makes it so much easier to insert cross-references while writing, without breaking the flow to search out the definition.<p>Rather than commenting out packages, I have two top-level input files, which `\input{}` the actual content.  Those files differ only where they read the developer-oriented definitions:<p><pre><code>    \input{latex_input&#x2F;defn&#x2F;dev_defns_for_devs.tex}
</code></pre>
versus<p><pre><code>    \input{latex_input&#x2F;defn&#x2F;dev_defns_for_release.tex}</code></pre>

If I open my text editor and type:<p><pre><code>    C:\&gt;
</code></pre>
I can make my editor look like an operating system.
